  "secciones": [
    {
      "id": "regla",
      "titulo": "The rule",
      "bloques": [
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "Instead of adding **pequeño** (_small_), Spanish often uses a diminutive suffix. With most words ending in **-e**, **-n** or **-r**, add **-cito**, **-cita**, **-citos**, **-citas**."
        },
        {
          "tipo": "tabla",
          "encabezados": [
            "Ending",
            "Example"
          ],
          "filas": [
            [
              "-e",
              "fuente → fuentecita, calle → callecita, suave → suavecito"
            ],
            [
              "-n",
              "camión → camioncito, Carmen → Carmencita"
            ],
            [
              "-r",
              "amor → amorcito, calor → calorcito"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "The suffix agrees in gender and number: **-cito**, **-cita**, **-citos**, **-citas**. Written accents disappear because the stress moves: **camión** → **camioncito**."
        },
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "It doesn't only mean small: it often adds **affection** or **softens** the word (**amorcito**, **ahorita**, **despacito**)."
        },
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "It works with nouns, adjectives and adverbs: **verdecito**, **suavecito**."
        },
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "Diminutives are used a lot in Mexico."
        },
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "Tip: **-cito** is a hug for the word."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
